# Conclusion

This notebook demonstrated usage of the Mitelman BigQuery dataset, which includes CytoConverter chromosomal coordinate data, in combination with TCGA BigQuery tables for a comparative analysis. Specifically, the notebook computes correlation (Pearson) coefficients between gains and losses obtained with Mitelam DB and TCGA datasets.

We observed that the mayority (but not all) of the significan correlation shown in Denomy et al. paper (Table 1, https://doi.org/10.1158/0008-5472.CAN-19-0585) are also significan in this analysis.
The table structure information is ({database name: {schema name: [table name]}}): 
{'mitelman-db': {'prod': ['AuthorReference', 'CytoConvertedLog', 'KaryBreak', 'CytoConverted', 'KaryBit', 'Koder', 'RecurrentNumData', 'CytoBands_hg38', 'KaryClone', 'Cytogen', 'MolClinGene', 'RecurrentData', 'KaryAbnorm', 'MolClinBreak', 'Reference', 'MolClinAbnorm', 'CytogenInv', 'MolBiolClinAssoc', 'CytogenInvValid']}}
Begin Exploring Related Columns
Below you find  

1) A short mapping of the columns that matter for the requested comparison, with the typical filters that will be applied, and  

2) Ten exploratory BigQuery SQL statements (from very simple to fairly complex) that show how to pull/aggregate the relevant information.  
All tables referenced belong to the dataset that was listed (`mitelman-db.prod`).  Each query is preceded by a short explanation and returns at most 20 rows.

────────────────────────────────────────  
Relevant columns / how they are used  
────────────────────────────────────────  

• `Cytogen.RefNo`, `Cytogen.CaseNo` – primary keys for a case; will be joined to `CytoConverted` to know which converted segments belong to the case.  
• `Cytogen.Morph` – filter; keep only `"3111"` (breast carcinoma).  
• `Cytogen.Topo` – filter; keep only `"0401"` (breast – ductal / adenocarcinoma).  

• `CytoConverted.RefNo`, `CytoConverted.CaseNo` – join‑keys back to `Cytogen`.  
• `CytoConverted.Chr` / `CytoConverted.ChrOrd` – chromosome designation / numeric order (will be grouped on).  
• `CytoConverted.Type` – copy‑number category per segment; values include `'Amp'` (amplification), `'Gain'`, `'Loss'`, `'Del'`/`'Deletion'`.  

• `RecurrentNumData.Chromosome`, `RecurrentNumData.Abnormality`, `RecurrentNumData.TotalCases` – an already aggregated (per chromosome, abnormality, morph, topo) view that can be treated as a “TCGA‑like” frequency table for demo purposes.  

────────────────────────────────────────  
Ten sample queries  
────────────────────────────────────────  

```sql
-- 1) Quick look at breast‑cancer cases (Morph 3111, Topo 0401)
SELECT *
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE Morph = '3111'
  AND Topo  = '0401'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 2) Distinct (RefNo, CaseNo) that satisfy the same filter
SELECT DISTINCT RefNo, CaseNo
FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE Morph = '3111'
  AND Topo  = '0401'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Chromosomes represented in CytoConverted for the filtered cases
SELECT DISTINCT cc.Chr
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS c
  ON cc.RefNo  = c.RefNo
 AND cc.CaseNo = c.CaseNo
WHERE c.Morph = '3111'
  AND c.Topo  = '0401'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Total number of *unique cases* in the cohort
SELECT COUNT(DISTINCT FORMAT('%d-%s', RefNo, CaseNo)) AS total_cases
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
WHERE  Morph = '3111'
  AND  Topo  = '0401'
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Counts of 'Gain' events per chromosome in the cohort
SELECT cc.ChrOrd, cc.Chr, COUNT(DISTINCT FORMAT('%d-%s', cc.RefNo, cc.CaseNo)) AS gain_cases
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS c
  ON cc.RefNo  = c.RefNo
 AND cc.CaseNo = c.CaseNo
WHERE  c.Morph = '3111'
  AND  c.Topo  = '0401'
  AND  cc.Type = 'Gain'
GROUP BY cc.ChrOrd, cc.Chr
ORDER BY cc.ChrOrd
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Counts of 'Loss' OR 'Del' events per chromosome in the cohort
SELECT cc.ChrOrd, cc.Chr,
       COUNT(DISTINCT FORMAT('%d-%s', cc.RefNo, cc.CaseNo)) AS loss_cases
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS c
  ON cc.RefNo  = c.RefNo
 AND cc.CaseNo = c.CaseNo
WHERE c.Morph = '3111'
  AND c.Topo  = '0401'
  AND cc.Type IN ('Loss','Del','Deletion')
GROUP BY cc.ChrOrd, cc.Chr
ORDER BY cc.ChrOrd
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Frequency (percentage of cohort) for the four CNA categories per chromosome
WITH cohort AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT FORMAT('%d-%s', RefNo, CaseNo) AS case_id
  FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
  WHERE Morph = '3111' AND Topo = '0401'
),
by_chr_type AS (
  SELECT cc.ChrOrd, cc.Chr, cc.Type,
         COUNT(DISTINCT FORMAT('%d-%s', cc.RefNo, cc.CaseNo)) AS n_cases
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
  JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS c
    ON cc.RefNo  = c.RefNo
   AND cc.CaseNo = c.CaseNo
  WHERE c.Morph = '3111'
    AND c.Topo  = '0401'
    AND cc.Type IN ('Amp','Gain','Loss','Del','Deletion')
  GROUP BY cc.ChrOrd, cc.Chr, cc.Type
)
SELECT ChrOrd, Chr, Type,
       n_cases,
       ROUND(100.0 * n_cases / (SELECT COUNT(*) FROM cohort), 2) AS freq_pct
FROM by_chr_type
ORDER BY ChrOrd, Type
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Create a compact table that keeps only rows with ≥5 cases
WITH cohort AS (
  SELECT DISTINCT FORMAT('%d-%s', RefNo, CaseNo) AS case_id
  FROM `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`
  WHERE Morph = '3111' AND Topo = '0401'
),
agg AS (
  SELECT cc.Chr, cc.Type,
         COUNT(DISTINCT FORMAT('%d-%s', cc.RefNo, cc.CaseNo)) AS n_cases
  FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.CytoConverted` AS cc
  JOIN   `mitelman-db.prod.Cytogen`       AS c
    ON cc.RefNo  = c.RefNo
   AND cc.CaseNo = c.CaseNo
  WHERE c.Morph = '3111'
    AND c.Topo  = '0401'
    AND cc.Type IN ('Amp','Gain','Loss','Del','Deletion')
  GROUP BY cc.Chr, cc.Type
)
SELECT *
FROM   agg
WHERE  n_cases >= 5
ORDER BY Chr, Type
LIMIT 20;
```

```sql
-- 9) "TCGA‑like" frequency taken from RecurrentNumData
-- (restricted to the same morph & topo and only whole‑chromosome +/− abnormalities)
SELECT Chromosome,
       Abnormality,   -- e.g. '+1', '-1'
       CAST(TotalCases AS INT64) AS tcga_cases
FROM   `mitelman-db.prod.RecurrentNumData`
